 ## Subtopics
 
-### Semantic Segmentation for Transient Objects
-Uses an integrated semantic segmentation model to generate masks for moving objects (pedestrians, cars) [S2]. This approach explicitly excludes these regions from scene training to mitigate depth map gaps [S2]. It is chosen when external segmentation priors are available, differing from the learnable U-Net approach by relying on pre-computed masks [S2].
-
-### Learnable Visibility Map
-Uses a U-Net to learn the visibility map end-to-end during training [S1]. This is chosen for unconstrained collections where pre-defined masks are unavailable, allowing the model to adaptively learn which regions are transient based on photometric consistency [S1].
+### Isolated Transient Network
+A separate network $T_\phi$ operating at the 2D image level to model transient objects (occluders) and their opacity, preventing noisy gradients from flowing into pose parameters [S3, unofficial]. It maps deep features $F_i(r)$ to transient opacity $\alpha^{(\tau)}$ and latent code $z^{(\tau)}$ using a sub-network $T_{\phi1}$ [S3, unofficial]. Another sub-network $T_{\phi2}$ encodes transient color $\hat{C}_i^{(\tau)}$ and uncertainty $\tilde{\beta}_i$ using the latent code and transient embedding $\ell_i^{(\tau)}$ [S3, unofficial]. The final color is alpha-blended: $\hat{C}_i(r) = (1 - \alpha^{(\tau)}(r))\hat{C}_i^{(a)}(r) + \alpha^{(\tau)}(r)\hat{C}_i^{(\tau)}(r)$ [S3, unofficial]. It uses parameters $\beta_{min}=0.1$ (minimum uncertainty) and $\lambda_\alpha=1.0$ (weight to suppress transient opacity) [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Adds the inference time of the U-Net or segmentation model for generating $V_M$ per training step [G]. The loss computation itself is $O(H \times W)$ due to the Hadamard product and L2 norms [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the visibility map $V_M$ of size $H \times W$ per image [G]. The U-Net or segmentation model weights are also loaded into memory [G].
-- **Measured Performance:** The method outperforms previous methods (including 3DGS) on PhotoTourism scenes (Brandenburg, Gate, Sacre Coeur, Trevi, Fountain) in terms of PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Effectively reduces artifacts from moving objects (floating points) and improves generalization by preventing the reconstruction of non-static geometry [S1, S2].
-- **Limitations:** Relies on the accuracy of the visibility map generation (U-Net or semantic segmentation). If the visibility map is inaccurate, it may either retain artifacts or remove valid static details [S1, S2]. The regularization weight is not specified in the sources [S1].
-- **When to Use:** Suitable for unconstrained image collections with significant transient objects where standard NeRF/Gaussian Splatting methods produce artifacts [S1].
-- **When Not to Use:** Not necessary for static scenes with no moving objects, as it adds computational overhead without benefit [G].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Adds the inference time of the segmentation model (U-Net) or transient network per frame if computed dynamically. If pre-computed, the overhead is negligible during training [S1, S2][S3, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the visibility map $V_M$ ($H \times W$ floats) or the parameters of the transient network [S1][S3, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Effectively removes artifacts from moving objects without requiring explicit 3D modeling of dynamics [S1, S2]. Improves depth map consistency by excluding inconsistent regions [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the accuracy of the segmentation/transient detection model. If the model fails to detect a transient object, artifacts may persist [S1, S2]. The regularization term requires careful tuning to prevent visibility collapse [S1].
+*   **When to use:** Unconstrained image collections with significant transient content (e.g., street scenes with pedestrians) [S1, S2].
+*   **When not to use:** Static scenes with no transient objects, where the overhead is unnecessary [S2].
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from "Gaussian in the Wild: 3D Gaussian Splatting for Unconstrained Image Collections" [S1], which addresses the challenge of reconstructing scenes from unstructured image sets containing dynamic elements. It builds upon the concept of visibility-based photometric loss, extending it to handle transience explicitly [S1]. An alternative approach using semantic segmentation for transient object handling is described in "UE4-NeRF: Neural Radiance Field for Real-Time Rendering of Large-Scale Scene" [S2], highlighting the broader context of handling dynamic objects in radiance field optimization [S2].
+The concept of handling transient objects in NeRF and 3D Gaussian Splatting emerged to address the limitation of these methods in assuming static scenes. Early works like NeRF-W introduced uncertainty modeling to handle transients [S3, unofficial]. Subsequent works integrated semantic segmentation masks to explicitly exclude moving objects [S2]. The visibility map approach, as detailed in "Gaussian in the Wild," applies this concept to 3D Gaussian Splatting for unconstrained image collections, using a U-Net to generate the map [S1]. The Isolated Transient Network variant was proposed in UP-NeRF to decouple transient modeling from pose optimization [S3, unofficial].
 
